<ul><li><a href="https://www.fao.org/worldfoodsituation/csdb/en/">FAO Cereal Supply and Demand Brief</a>, released 2 October 2026 — production, supply, utilization, trade and stock estimates.</li><li><a href="https://www.fao.org/newsroom/detail/fao-food-price-index-rises-in-september-amid-weather-concerns-and-transport-disruptions/en">FAO Food Price Index update</a>, 2 October 2026 — international price context.</li><li><a href="https://www.wfp.org/publications/global-report-food-crises-grfc">Global Report on Food Crises 2026</a> — acute food insecurity in assessed crisis countries and territories.</li></ul>
<p>The central message is measured: global cereal output is forecast to remain historically high, but a large harvest is not a guarantee of affordable, safe and reliable food access in every place. The best assessment combines production with stocks, trade, prices and the conditions households face.</p><section class="article-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><h2 id="faq-title">Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is the global cereal production forecast for 2026?</h3><p>FAO's 2 October 2026 Cereal Supply and Demand Brief forecast 2.979 billion tonnes, described as the second-largest harvest on record. It is a forecast and may be revised.</p><h3>Does a large cereal harvest mean food is affordable everywhere?</h3><p>No. Affordability depends on income, local prices, transport, market access, conflict and whether supplies reach a particular area. Aggregate output cannot show every household's situation.</p><h3>Why are cereal stocks important?</h3><p>Stocks can buffer the period between harvests and help markets absorb shocks. The interpretation depends on the crop, stocks-to-use ratio, geography and the portion available for trade.</p><h3>Where can I find updated global cereal data?</h3><p>Use FAO's Cereal Supply and Demand Brief at fao.org/worldfoodsituation/csdb and check each release's date, revisions and definitions.</p></section><div class="article-end"><p><strong>Continue reading</strong></p><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-link" href="/articles/food-prices-vs-shortages/"><small>Food prices</small><strong>Are high food prices proof of a global shortage?</strong></a><a class="related-link" href="/articles/food-security-vs-availability/"><small>Core concepts</small><strong>Food security vs. food availability</strong></a></div><p class="source-note">This article is informational, not a real-time emergency alert. For current conditions, consult the primary sources linked above and local official guidance. <a href="/methodology/">Read our methodology</a>